[2026-09-30T10:11:09.833Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"reasoning","text":"**Deciding upgrade scope**\n\nI need to determine the scope to ensure we can satisfactorily address the possible issues without closing them completely. I could upgrade OpenTUI to the latest version .13 to tackle security and npm audit findings. It might be a good idea to check for outdated dependencies right after installation. I should probably write a bash parser to support versions .25 and .27 while keeping one stable. I'll aim to conduct meaningful tests and use an isolated environment for version .27.\n**Considering parser compatibility**\n\nI see that maintainers have warned that fallback support isn't available for OpenTUI, but I can test the bash versions. Extracting the parser module to create a dual-version test script could be useful, and I might need to automate tests to verify them against the actual tool, although I faced issues with version .25. I'll implement runtime introspection to work with the compatible .27 version while referring to the documentation. It feels like I need to balance the scope without making it too overwhelming.\n**Planning reproducible experiments**\n\nI’m thinking about creating an experiment script that packs the agent to install both versions .25 and .27 side by side, using a manifest to direct the dependency alias. While the full dependency might be 400MB x2, that shouldn't be too much for the host. I could modify the `js/node_modules/web-tree-sitter` via bun with a temporary root package clone. Also, it might be interesting to copy necessary files to experiment with symlinking dependencies. There’s some complexity to this approach, but it could be worthwhile."}}
